---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/data';
---
<Base title="How we verify" description="The four review layers every update must pass before it is published.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>How we verify</h1>
    <p class="lede">Drafting is done with the help of a language model. Language models can invent things, so nothing reaches this site
      on the model's word. Every update must pass four independent layers of checks. Anything that fails is held back,
      logged publicly and sent to a human.</p>

    <h2>Where facts come from</h2>
    <p>We monitor official sources daily: EUR-Lex and the EU Publications Office, the Commission's <em>Have Your Say</em> portal,
      Parliament's Legislative Observatory, Council press releases, DG Environment, ECHA, EFSA, UNEP, the Basel Convention,
      WTO ePing, the US Federal Register, state legislatures, GOV.UK, and national governments and statutory bodies. Trade
      associations and media are used only as <strong>leads</strong>: every fact on the site must trace to an official source.
      Each document we rely on is saved as a text snapshot with its URL, retrieval time and SHA-256 hash in the
      <a href="https://github.com/rethinksci-gif/plastics-policy-tracker/tree/main/data/raw">public archive</a>.</p>

    <h2>Layer 1 — deterministic checks (code)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every record is validated against a strict schema.</li>
      <li>Every source link must return HTTP 200 and be on our whitelist of official domains.</li>
      <li>Dates must make sense: an event that has happened cannot be dated after today; next steps whose date has passed are marked <em>to be confirmed</em>.</li>
      <li>Stage changes must be legal: stages only move forward. A backwards move, a jump of more than two stages, or a move to <em>withdrawn</em>/<em>stalled</em> is sent to a human.</li>
      <li>The same event cannot be recorded twice in a dossier.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Layer 2 — source provenance (code)</h2>
    <p>Every update carries a <strong>verbatim quote</strong> from its source. After normalising whitespace, quotation marks and letter case,
      the quote must be found in the saved snapshot of the cited page. If it is not there, the update is rejected outright.
      This is the main defence against invented content. For sources in other languages we keep the original quote and show an English translation next to it.</p>

    <h2>Layer 3 — an independent reviewer (separate model call)</h2>
    <p>A separate reviewer call sees <em>only</em> the source text and the claim, never the drafter's reasoning. It is told to look for
      errors and to assume the claim is wrong until the source proves it. It checks dates, numbers, institutions, legal references,
      stage changes and next steps, and looks for overstatement or inference that the source does not contain. Only a verdict of
      <code>supported</code> with confidence ≥ 0.80 passes. Items about high-impact instruments that pass then go to a
      second, independent reviewer running on a stronger model, and must pass both. The model used for each review is
      shown in its review record.</p>

    <h2>Layer 4 — editorial review of the whole issue</h2>
    <p>Before an issue goes out, the full set of items is checked together: contradictions between items or with a dossier's history,
      missing high-impact developments, consistent terminology and British spelling, faithful translations, and a strict
      separation of fact from interpretation. Interpretation only ever appears in boxes labelled <em>Analysis</em>.
      Only factual problems hold an item back; wording and house-style points are listed in the review report for the
      editor but never stop publication.</p>

    <h2>Which model does what</h2>
    <p>Screening sources, drafting and the first review run on a fast model (DeepSeek Flash). Code, not a model, checks
      the facts most at risk of invention (quotes, dates, sources). A stronger model (DeepSeek V4 Pro) is used only for
      the second opinion on high-impact items and for the editorial review of each issue.</p>

    <h2>What happens when something fails</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It goes into <strong>quarantine</strong>: not published, recorded in the review report, and opened as a GitHub issue for a human to check.</li>
      <li><strong>Publish gate:</strong> if more than 30% of an issue's items are quarantined, or any high-impact item is quarantined,
        the issue is not published automatically. It waits for the editor to approve it by hand.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Review records</h2>
    <p>Every published update shows its source link, the quote (expandable) and a review ID linking to its full review record.
      Every issue links to its issue-level review report, including the quarantined items and the reasons. All records are
      also kept in the <a href="https://github.com/rethinksci-gif/plastics-policy-tracker/tree/main/reviews">repository</a>.</p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>Some official sites block automated access; these are listed in
      <a href="https://github.com/rethinksci-gif/plastics-policy-tracker/blob/main/sources/unreachable.md">unreachable.md</a>,
      and we do not substitute secondary sources for them. This is a monitoring tool, not legal advice; always rely on the official text.
      See also the <a href={url('about/')}>about page</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
